prompt engineering is in the first place.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.560
Prompt engineering in a nutshell is a career

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.640
that came about of the back of the rise

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.520
of artificial intelligence.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.760
It involves human writing, refining and optimizing prompts

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.440
in a structured way.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.320
This is done with the intention of perfecting the interaction

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.840
between humans and AI to the highest degree possible.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.200
Not only that, however, a prompt engineer is then also

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.680
required to continuously monitor those prompts

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.600
ensure their effectiveness with time as AI progresses.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.560
Maintaining an up-to-date prompt library will be a requirement

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.840
that is placed onto the prompt engineer

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.360
as well as reporting on findings and in general,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.460
being a thought leader in this space.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.120
But why do we need it?

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.380
And how did it come about from AI?

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.520
Before moving on, let's actually make sure

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.400
we are on the same page about what exactly AI is.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.480
Artificial intelligence is the simulation

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.760
of human intelligence processes by machines.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.720
I say simulation as artificial intelligence

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.760
is not sentient, at least not yet anyways,

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.960
meaning it cannot think for itself

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.320
as much as it may seem it does.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.920
Often, and this is certainly the case with tools

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.080
such as chat GPT, for example, when we say AI,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.660
we are simply referring to a term called machine learning.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.920
Machine learning works by using large amounts

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.440
of training data that is then analyzed

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.840
for correlations and patterns.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.200
These patterns are then used to predict outcomes

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.640
based on the training data provided.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.320
So for example, here we are feeding data

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.840
saying that if a paragraph looks like this

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.240
with this type of title, then it should be categorized

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.440
as international finance.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.760
The second paragraph should be put in the category

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.840
of earning reports and so on.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.000
With some code, we should be able to train our AI model

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.240
to correctly guess what future paragraphs are about.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.640
And that's it.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.480
Of course, that is a super basic example

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.360
and we would need way more data

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.320
than these five paragraphs right here,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.620
but you get the idea.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.300
If you want to build your own AI model

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.480
and understand the concept of machine learning better

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.160
as a total beginner, please check out my video on it

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.600
on my channel, Code with Anya Kubo.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.480
As of now, rapidly improving general AI techniques

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.480
can create realistic text responses

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.240
and even images, music, and other media

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.500
thanks to the huge amounts of training data

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.260
and talented developers working on it today.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.340
Why is prompt engineering useful?

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.400
With the quick and exponential growing rise of AI,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.800
even the architects of it themselves struggle

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.180
to control it and its outputs.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.200
This might be a bit hard to understand,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.880
but think of it this way.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.320
If you were to ask an AI chatbot what is four plus four,

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.720
you would expect it to say eight, right?

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.500
The result of eight is pretty indisputable.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.360
However, imagine you are a young student

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.640
trying to learn the English language.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.920
I'm going to show you just how different responses can be

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.080
based on the prompts you feed

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.680
and in turn your learning experience.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.400
For this example, I'm going to be using

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.520
Chat GPT's GPT-4 model.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.840
So let's start with the basics.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.840
If you were to type, correct my paragraph

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.020
and then paste it a badly written paragraph.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.900
So just like this, today was great in the world for me.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.660
I went to a Disneyland with my mom.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.620
It could have been better though if it wasn't raining.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.880
Great, the young English learner has a better sentence,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.280
but it kind of stops there

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.840
and the learner is just left to their own devices.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.000
And honestly, the sentence really isn't that great anyway.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.520
What if the learner could get the best sentences possible

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.280
from a teacher who understands their interests

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.020
to keep them engaged?

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.480
With the correct prompts,

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.080
we can actually create that with AI.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.640
So let's give it a go and let's write a prompt to do this.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.080
So here's the prompt I'm going to give it.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.080
I'm going to write,

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.320
I want you to act as a spoken English teacher.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.960
I will speak to you in English

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.800
and you'll reply to me in English

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.040
to practice my spoken English.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.120
I want you to keep my reply neat,

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.080
limiting the reply to 100 words.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.480
I also want you to strictly correct

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.720
my grammar mistakes and typos.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.960
And I want you to ask me a question in your reply.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.940
Now, let's start practicing.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:48.080
You could ask me a question first.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.520
Remember, I want you to strictly correct

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:52.900
my grammar mistakes and typos.

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:54.500
So there's my prompt.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.080
You can also go a step further

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.040
and ask it to correct your factual errors too,

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.660
which I think would be an excellent addition to the prompt

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:03.840
that will benefit the young learner.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.880
Okay, so let's go ahead and add that to the prompt.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.360
Okay, and let us do its thing.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.040
And great, so now this is way more interactive.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.560
As you will see, it's asking you a question

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.360
and telling you what to do

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.720
and will provide you with corrections if needed.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.240
So in a way, you're communicating with the AI.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.640
It's giving you suggestions

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.040
and you're learning along the way.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.520
It's a completely different experience

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.760
thanks to the prompt that we wrote.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:31.880
Pretty cool, right?

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.240
We're gonna be diving into a bunch of these concepts soon,

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:37.040
but first let's start with the basics.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:38.600
Linguistics.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.400
Linguistics is the study of language.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.000
It focuses on everything from phonetics,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.200
so the study of how speech sounds

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.960
are produced and perceived.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:51.880
Phonology, so the study of sound patterns and changes.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.600
Morphology, the study of word structure.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.840
Syntax, so the study of sentence structure.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.200
Semantics, so the study of linguistic meaning.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.800
Pragmatics, so in other words,

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.680
the study of how language is used in context.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.800
Historical linguistics, or the study of language change.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.320
Sociolinguistics, or in other words,

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.360
the study of the relation between language and society.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.640
Computational linguistics,

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.720
so the study of how computers can process human language.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.680
And physiolinguistics,

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.840
or the study of how humans acquire and use language.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.120
So a lot.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.080
Linguistics are the key to prompt engineering.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:33.080
Why?

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.480
Understanding the nuances of language

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:37.680
and how it is used in different contexts

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.760
is crucial for crafting effective prompts.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:41.680
Not only that,

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.200
but knowing how to use a grammar or language structure

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.800
that is universally used

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.520
will result in the AI system

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.400
returning back the most accurate results.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:51.360
As you can imagine,

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.000
the sheer amount of data that it is trained on

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.000
is most likely to mostly use the standard grammar

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.960
and language structure that is universally used.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.800
So sticking to the standardization is key.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.600
Language models.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.920
Imagine a world where computers possess the power

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:11.520
to understand and generate human language.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.320
A world where machines can chat, write stories,

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.520
and even compose poetry.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.320
In this magical realm,

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.200
language models can come into play.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.680
They are like the wizards of the digital realm

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.600
capable of understanding and creating human-like text.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.200
A language model is a clever computer program

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.160
that learns from a vast collection of written text.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.920
It takes in books, articles, websites,

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:38.960
and all sorts of written resources,

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.240
allowing it to gather knowledge

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.560
about how humans use language.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:44.000
Just like a master linguist,

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.520
it becomes an expert in the art of conversation,

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.360
grammar, and style.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.560
But how does this all work?

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:53.040
Well, imagine you feed a sentence.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.640
The language model will then analyze the sentence,

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:58.160
examine the order of words, their meanings,

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.840
and the way they fit together.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.040
Then the language model would generate a prediction

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.320
or a continuation of the sentence

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:08.560
that makes sense based on its understanding of the language.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.360
It will weave words together one by one,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.840
creating a response that seems like it was crafted

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.360
by a human being.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.080
Imagine having a conversation with the language model

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.920
as if you were exchanging ideas with a digital friend.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:22.160
You ask a question,

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.680
and it responds with a well-crafted answer.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.780
You tell a joke, and it counters with a witty remark.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.880
It's like having a language expert by your side

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.080
always ready to assist and engage in conversation.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.920
Now, you may wonder where these language models are used.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.240
They can be found in various places,

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.200
from your smartphone's virtual assistants

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.000
to customer service chatbots,

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.080
and even in the creative realm of writing.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.080
They help us find information,

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.720
offer suggestions, and create content.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:52.880
But remember, while language models

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.440
possess incredible abilities,

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:58.280
they still rely on the human to create and train them.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:00.960
They are, in fact, a fusion of human ingenuity

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.680
and the power of algorithms,

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:05.440
blending the best of both worlds.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.040
Let's start off by looking at the history of language models,

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.320
starting with the first AI, Eliza, back in the 60s.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.120
Eliza is an early natural language processing computer

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.120
program created from 1964 to 1966 at MIT by Joseph Wiesenbaum.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.160
Eliza was designed to simulate a conversation

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:26.640
with a human being.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:28.440
Eliza had a special knack for mimicking

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.720
a Rogerian psychotherapist,

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.280
someone who essentially listens attentively

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.120
and asks probing questions

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.920
to help people explore their thoughts and feelings.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.080
Eliza's secret weapon was its mastery of pattern matching.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:44.880
It had a treasure trove of predefined patterns,

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.880
each associated with specific responses.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.040
These patterns were like magical spells

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.800
that allowed Eliza to understand

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.200
and respond to human language.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:56.480
When you engage in a conversation with Eliza,

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:58.400
it would carefully analyze your input,

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.600
seeking patterns and keywords.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:01.920
It would then transform your words

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:03.640
into a series of symbols,

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:06.160
searching for patterns that match those symbols

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.600
in its repertoire.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.160
Once a pattern was detected,

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:12.240
Eliza would work its magic by transforming your words

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.520
into a question or statement

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.240
that aimed to explore your thoughts and emotions.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:20.440
It was as if Eliza was holding up a metaphorical mirror,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:24.080
encouraging you to delve deeper into your own thinking.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.680
For example, if you said something like, I feel sad,

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:29.120
Eliza would detect the pattern

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:31.360
and respond with a question like,

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.600
why do you think you feel sad?

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.200
It encouraged reflection and introspection,

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.080
just like a caring therapist would.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.480
But here's the delightful twist.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.600
Eliza didn't truly understand what you were saying.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.600
It was just a clever illusion.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:48.360
It used pattern matching

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.960
and some creative programming tricks

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.360
to create the illusion of understanding

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:54.440
while in reality,

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:57.720
it was just following a set of predefined rules.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.720
Yet, even though Eliza was a simple program,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.400
people were often captivated

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:04.600
by its conversational abilities.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:06.480
They felt heard and understood,

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.480
even if they knew they were talking to a machine.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.360
It was like having a digital confident

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.040
who was always ready to listen and offer gentle guidance.

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.400
Eliza's creator, Weisenbaum,

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.160
intended the program as a method to explore communication

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.000
between humans and machines.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.600
He was surprised and shocked that individuals,

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.360
including his secretary,

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:29.960
attributed human-like feelings to the computer program.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:34.040
But that is a whole other topic of conversation in itself.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.680
Eliza's impact was profound,

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:37.240
sparking interest and research

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.560
in the field of natural language processing.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.920
It paved the way for more advanced systems

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:45.720
that could truly understand and generate human language.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.280
It was the humble beginning of a grand adventure

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:51.040
in the world of conversational AI.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:52.800
Fast forward to the 1970s,

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.160
when a program named Shudlu appeared.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.800
It could understand simple commands

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:00.920
and interact with a virtual world of blocks.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:04.400
Although Shudlu wasn't a language model per se,

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.600
it laid the foundation for the idea of machines

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.760
comprehending human language.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:12.480
But the true language models began around 2010,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.960
when the power of deep learning

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.480
and neural networks came into play.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:19.240
Enter the mighty GPT,

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:22.440
short for generative pre-trained transformer,

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.960
ready to conquer the world of language.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:26.240
In the year 2018,

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.560
the fast iteration of GPT emerged,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.040
created by the company OpenAI.

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:34.360
It was trained on a large amount of text data,

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.640
absorbing knowledge from books, articles,

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.640
and a large chunk of the internet.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.080
GPT-1 was a taste of things to come,

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:42.640
an impressive language model,

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.280
but small compared to its descendants that we use today.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:47.120
As time went on,

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:51.560
the saga continued with the arrival of GPT-2 in 2019,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.560
followed by GPT-3 in 2020.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:57.080
This was a titan among language models

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.280
equipped with a large number of parameters,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:02.280
over 175 billion to be precise.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.000
GPT-3 dazzled the world

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.360
with its unparalleled ability to understand,

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.960
respond, and even generate creative pieces of writing.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:12.360
The arrival of GPT-3 marked a real turning point

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.040
in terms of language models and AI.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:16.040
At the time of writing,

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.080
we now also have GPT-4,

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.200
trained on pretty much the whole internet,

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:22.520
rather than outdated large data sets,

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.880
as well as BERT from Google and so much more.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:27.920
It would seem we are only just at the start

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.040
when it comes to language models and AI.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.920
So learning how to harness this data with prompt engineering

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.160
is a smart move for anyone today.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.360
The prompt engineering mindset.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:39.560
When thinking of good prompts,

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.680
it is always best to get in the correct mindset.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.840
Essentially, you want to just write one prompt, right?

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.320
And not have to waste time and tokens,

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:50.120
writing lots of different prompts

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:52.400
until you get the result you desire.

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:54.360
So essentially, kind of the same

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.160
as when you Google stuff, right?

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.480
How good are your Googling skills now,

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:00.400
as opposed to five years ago?

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.240
I'm assuming a lot better.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:04.800
We have grown to intuitively know

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:07.520
what to type into Google the first time round

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.360
as to not waste time.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.800
Having the same mindset for prompt engineering

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.800
can also be applied.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.360
Mahail Eric of the Infinite Machine Learning Podcast

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.040
says it well when he says,

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.520
I personally like the analogy of prompting

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.040
to designing effective Google searches.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.000
There are clearly better and worse ways to write queries

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.600
against the Google search engine that solve your task.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.400
This variance exists because of the opaqueness

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:35.000
of what Google is doing under the hood.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.680
We are gonna keep this in mind

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:39.360
for the remainder of the course.

